ChatSched — Claude fixes, Sept 5
=================================

Scope: this file documents ONLY the fifth task resolved from
"Claude To fix 1..txt" — item 9, HIGH: Business-to-publisher
communication conflicts with the new agency model. Items 5, 6, 7, and 8
were already completed and documented separately ("claude fixes Sept 1"
through "Sept 4"). Items 10 onward were left untouched.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. What the finding said, and what I actually found when I checked it
------------------------------------------------------------------------
The finding names four affected files and says the current business
model (Business -> ChatSched -> Publisher) conflicts with the codebase
still supporting direct, unmediated 1:1 business<->publisher
conversations, "opened by Contact Publisher" per schema_phase29's own
comment.

I read all four files before writing anything, and found the actual
picture was more mixed than the finding's own affected-files list
suggests:

  Still a real, live problem:
    conversations_insert_business (schema_phase29_conversations.sql)
    lets ANY authenticated business open a brand-new conversations row
    with ANY approved publisher, with no requirement that a campaign
    request or booking exists at all. This is exactly the unmediated
    "Business direct to Publisher" path the current model is meant to
    prevent, and it's real: confirmed by reading the policy's own
    `with check` clause directly.

  Already fixed, not actually broken (confirmed by reading the current
  code, not assumed from the finding's file list):
    - src/pages/PublisherProfile.tsx has NO "Contact Publisher" CTA
      anywhere, for either booking flow. It already says "Start Campaign
      Request" everywhere, inserts into `requests` (never `conversations`),
      and its non-request-flow branch already carries "ChatSched handles
      the publisher relationship: briefing, scheduling, verification, and
      escrow" copy with a "Managed" badge. I checked this file
      exhaustively (grepped every "message"/"conversation" mention in it)
      and found zero references to the messaging system at all. This
      page needed no code change; it's only in this writeup because the
      finding named it.
    - There is no live UI link anywhere in this codebase pointing at
      `/messages?publisher=...` (confirmed by grep across all of src/) —
      the literal "Contact Publisher" entry point schema_phase29's header
      comment describes has already been fully removed from the visible
      UI. The capability was still reachable at the database/API level
      regardless of any button existing for it, which is why closing it
      only in the UI was never going to be a real fix on its own — see
      below.
    - 11 of the 12 channel definitions (src/channels/*) already use
      `bookingFlow: "request"`, meaning channel_requests with no direct
      messaging involved at all. Only the original social-media channel
      still uses `bookingFlow: "directory"`, and even that one already
      routes through the same "Start Campaign Request" -> `requests`
      table flow as every other channel, with the same "ChatSched
      manages this" framing in its own copy.

This confirms the actual bug isn't "a visible Contact Publisher button
somewhere" (already gone) — it's that the underlying `conversations`
table's INSERT policy still allowed the thing the button used to trigger,
reachable by anyone calling the Supabase API directly regardless of
what's in the UI.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. A separate, mediated messaging system already exists and was not
   touched
------------------------------------------------------------------------
While tracing this I found the codebase already has a SECOND, entirely
separate messaging system: a `messages` table
(schema_phase56_channel_messaging.sql) scoped to a specific
`request_id`/`channel_request_id` — i.e., chat tied to an actual
campaign/booking, surfaced via a `MessageThread` component inside
PublisherDashboardView.tsx and CampaignWorkspace.tsx. This is the
already-correct, campaign-scoped, mediated alternative to the free-
standing `conversations` table this task retires. Nothing about this
second system needed any change — it was never the problem, and I left
it completely alone.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Files changed
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- supabase/schema_phase85_retire_direct_messaging.sql (NEW)
    - Drops conversations_insert_business.
    - Creates conversations_insert_admin: `for insert with check
      (public.is_admin() and auth.uid() = business_id)`. This keeps the
      original policy's own `auth.uid() = business_id` requirement
      exactly as it was (so even an admin can't set business_id to
      someone else's real id — they can only open a new conversation
      appearing as themselves), and simply requires is_admin() as an
      additional condition. The net effect: only ChatSched (an admin
      session) can open a brand-new conversations row now; an ordinary
      business cannot. Existing conversations, and both
      conversation_messages policies (which govern sending within an
      already-open thread), are completely untouched — this closes the
      door on new unmediated contact without retroactively touching
      anything that already exists.

- src/pages/Messages.tsx (EDITED)
    - Removed the entire dead `/messages?publisher=` auto-create-
      conversation block (the useEffect that called
      findOrCreateConversation, plus its opening/openError state) — this
      code was already unreachable via any UI link, and after the DB fix
      it would only ever produce a permission-denied error for a
      business, so removing it outright (matching the instruction to
      "retire" this path) is cleaner than leaving dead, now-guaranteed-
      to-fail code in place.
    - Updated three pieces of copy that the DB fix made false: the page
      intro ("Talk to publishers and businesses here" -> "Direct notes
      from the ChatSched team..."), the empty-state description
      ("Contact a publisher from their listing to start a conversation
      here" -> pointing at the dashboard/campaign workspace instead,
      where campaign-scoped messaging actually lives), and the "no
      conversation selected" placeholder text. Left everything else
      (loading an existing conversation, the message composer, realtime
      updates) completely unchanged — none of it assumed a business
      could create new threads.

- src/lib/conversations.ts (DELETED)
    - findOrCreateConversation() had exactly one caller
      (Messages.tsx's now-removed block); confirmed via grep that
      nothing else in the codebase imports this file before deleting it.
      Its entire purpose was letting a business cold-start a conversation
      with a publisher — precisely the capability this task retires, so
      keeping an unused-but-working version of it around would be
      exactly the kind of "still technically reachable" residual risk
      this whole finding is about.

- supabase/tests/retire_direct_messaging_test.sql (NEW)
    - pgTAP test file, 5 assertions. Proves: an ordinary business can no
      longer open a new conversation with a publisher it has no existing
      thread with; that same business can still send a message in a
      conversation that already existed before this fix (and so can the
      publisher on that same thread) — confirming existing threads are
      untouched; an admin can still open a brand-new conversation,
      appearing as the business_id side themselves, keeping publisher-
      ChatSched communication alive; and even an admin can't open one
      with business_id set to a real business's id instead of their own.

- supabase/tests/README.md (EDITED)
    - Added a section documenting how to run
      retire_direct_messaging_test.sql and what it covers.

- Claude To fix 1..txt (EDITED)
    - Marked item 9 as [COMPLETED], pointing at the new migration, the
      new test file, and this document.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Why this shape, not a different one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Tightened the INSERT policy rather than dropping the `conversations`/
  `conversation_messages` tables outright, because the finding explicitly
  says to "keep publisher <-> ChatSched communication" — the existing
  design already supported an admin account being the business_id side
  of a conversation (the original policy's own `role in ('business',
  'admin')` check), so the mechanism for ChatSched-to-publisher contact
  was already there; only the ordinary-business branch needed removing.
- Left existing conversations and message-sending completely untouched
  rather than also trying to lock/hide old threads, because the finding
  asks to retire the pattern going forward (stop the bypass), not to
  retroactively delete data — and I have no way to know from this
  sandbox whether any real conversations exist in a live deployment or
  what forcibly cutting off an active thread (support-related or
  otherwise) would break. Closing the door on NEW unmediated contact is
  the actual "bypass risk" named in the finding; anything beyond that is
  a separate, more destructive decision I wasn't asked to make.
- Did not modify PublisherProfile.tsx, because it already has nothing to
  fix — confirmed by direct inspection, not assumed from the finding's
  own file list. Listing it as "checked, found already correct" felt
  more honest than silently skipping it or, worse, changing something
  that didn't need changing just because the finding named the file.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. What's NOT done, and why that's a deliberate boundary here
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Items 10 through 13 in "Claude To fix 1..txt" are untouched.
- Claude to fix 2.txt and Claude to fix 3.txt were not opened.
- I did not build any new "ChatSched reaches out to a publisher"
  admin UI — conversations_insert_admin makes this possible at the
  database level now, but no admin page currently calls it (nothing did
  before this fix either, since the only prior caller was the business-
  facing Messages.tsx flow that's now removed). That's a genuine gap if
  ChatSched staff ever want to proactively message a publisher through
  this system, but building that tool wasn't part of this finding.
- Same standing honesty note this project's test suite has carried since
  it began: no Postgres, Docker, or Supabase CLI in this sandbox, so
  neither the new migration nor the new test file has been run against a
  real database. Everything here was reviewed by hand, line-by-line,
  against the actual current policy definitions and every real UI path
  in the codebase — not written from memory of what they "should" say.
  The first real `supabase test db` run is the actual test of whether
  this is correct.
